Deep Learning Practical: Fashion Image Classification

A Google Colab / Jupyter notebook practical for BBA AI/ML students, teaching the basics of deep learning through an e-commerce image classification use case.

Business Scenario

An e-commerce company receives thousands of product images every day. Instead of manually tagging each one, a deep learning model can classify images into categories such as:

T-shirt / Top
Trouser
Pullover
Dress
Coat
Sandal
Shirt
Sneaker
Bag
Ankle Boot
What's in the Notebook

Deep_Learning_Fashion_Image_Classification_BBA.ipynb walks through the full pipeline:

Import required libraries (TensorFlow/Keras, Matplotlib)
Load the Fashion MNIST dataset (downloads automatically — no manual upload needed)
Define the 10 product categories
Visualize sample product images
Normalize pixel values (0–255 → 0–1)
Build a simple neural network (Flatten → Dense(64, relu) → Dense(10, softmax))
Compile and train the model (3 epochs, for a quick classroom demo)
Evaluate test accuracy
Generate predictions on unseen test images
Inspect individual predictions (image_number can be changed to try other images)

Each technical step is paired with a business-interpretation note connecting the ML concept to a real e-commerce workflow (e.g., what accuracy means for deployment risk, human review, and customer experience).

Requirements
Python 3
TensorFlow / Keras
Matplotlib
NumPy

Runs out of the box in Google Colab; no local setup or dataset download required.

Usage
Open the notebook in Google Colab (or Jupyter).
Run all cells top to bottom.
Change image_number in Step 13 to test predictions on different images.
Complete the Student Activity and Final Reflection sections at the end.
Learning Objectives
Understand the input → hidden layer → output structure of a neural network
Understand training vs. testing, and what "accuracy" means in practice
Connect a technical ML pipeline to a real business process and its risks/benefits
Practice interpreting model predictions and considering human oversight in AI-assisted workflows
